// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    Flashback
// @category Flashbacks and Chronology
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/Flashback_(trope)
// @domain   Narrative
// @version  1.3
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): a narrative technique in which we are shown events that took place
// before the main action — the story cuts AWAY from the present and BACK in time, then returns.
// A trigger in the present (a line, an object, a face) opens the past; an idiosyncratic wipe or
// dissolve marks the crossing. FRONTIER: where Dream Sequence opens a `non_canon` plane and Frame
// Story nests two canon tellers, Flashback names the bare TEMPORAL crossing — an EARLIER, CANON
// sub-level (it really happened; cf. the dream's unreality) that the narrative dives INTO and
// climbs back OUT of. Modeled on the S14 level axis (a depicted, narrated, watched embedded scene
// — a crossing), NOT a `past scene {}` window (a backward QUERY within one plane, used by
// Foreshadowing's payoff). The remembering character is the level's `teller`; the level stays
// `canon`, which is exactly what separates a flashback from a dream.


// === IMPORTS & ASSOCIATIONS ===
import "trl/concepts/index.trl"            // memory, the_past, revelation

assoc flashback -> [
  memory     : "surfaces a",
  the_past   : "depicts the",
  revelation : "often a delayed",
  frame_story : "a temporal sibling of the",
  dream_sequence : "canon, unlike the"
]


// === ONTOLOGY ===

imply Flashback -> [Nested, Anachronic, Narrative]


// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Naming rule: when a present trigger makes a character recall an earlier scene — the story cutting
// back in time — that act of recall IS a Flashback (tags the event). The remembered tale sits one
// level DOWN in time (the canon past plane); the crossing INTO it and the return are loud by S14.
rule Flashback {
  when:
    evt $f [&Recalls(agent=$rememberer)]
  then:
    $f [+Flashback]
}


// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: Casablanca — "As Time Goes By" cuts to Paris (film, 1942) ===
// In Rick's Café, Ilsa asks Sam to play the song they shared; the music triggers Rick, and the
// film dissolves to their Paris romance before the German occupation — a canon flashback that
// really happened — then returns to the present-day café. Two planes, flat and ambient: `casa`
// (the framing present, Casablanca 1941) and `casa.paris` (the earlier Paris love affair, told
// from Rick's memory). The past plane is CANON; the crossing back in time and the return are loud.

// ===== the framing present — Rick's Café Américain, the night Ilsa walks in =====
<<casa>>

// RICK BLAINE
char rick [+Protagonist] [+Cynical]
// ILSA LUND
char ilsa [+LoveInterest]
// SAM
char sam [+Musician]
// THE SONG — "As Time Goes By", the trigger object
obj the_song [+Trigger]

// INT. RICK'S CAFÉ AMÉRICAIN - NIGHT
set cafe [+Interior] [+Night]

// ===== the earlier Paris romance — one level down in time, told from Rick's memory; still canon =====
<<casa.paris : teller=rick>>

// EXT. PARIS - DAY (before the occupation)
set paris [+Exterior] [+Day]
// rick and ilsa are the present (casa) characters recalled into the Paris memory — a loud crossing (S14 §7)
<<casa|rick>> -- <<casa|ilsa>> : "the lovers they were, before the war took it"

// ===== reopen the present — the dissolve that opens the flashback, and the return =====
<<casa>>

scene the_dissolve {
  beat 1 {
    sam @ cafe
    ilsa @ cafe
    ilsa > the_song
    sam -- the_song : "Plays"
  }
  beat 2 {
    rick @ cafe
    rick > the_song
    // the song opens the past: Rick recalls Paris — the film cuts back in time
    evt remember [&Recalls(agent=rick, scene=<<casa.paris|paris>>)]
    // Flashback -> remember [+Flashback]; the narrative dives DOWN into casa.paris, then returns
  }
  beat 3 {
    // back in the present café — the flashback ends, the music stops, Rick is changed by it
    rick @ cafe
    rick [#Haunted(source=the_song)]
  }
}
